---
/**
 * The privacy policy — every locale, one file.
 *
 * ── The bug this route exists to fix ────────────────────────────────────────
 * On the old site the policy was `src/pages/muqun/privacy.md`, and there was no
 * `src/pages/[lang]/muqun/privacy` beside it. Both Muqun pages linked to it
 * through `localizedPathForLocale('/muqun/privacy', locale)`, which happily
 * produced `/ja/muqun/privacy`, `/de/muqun/privacy` and seven more addresses
 * that had never existed. Nine of the ten locales rendered a link to a 404 —
 * from the promise block, whose whole argument is that this app can be trusted
 * with your machine, and from the support page's safe-reporting notice. It is
 * fixed here by construction: this route enumerates the same locale list as the
 * two pages that link to it, so a locale cannot have one without the other.
 * ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The body stays in English in every locale. It is the document Apple and
 * Google review and the one a reader would take a dispute to, so it has one
 * authoritative text rather than eight that can drift apart at a clause. The
 * frame around it — title, revision date, and the sentence saying which text
 * governs — is translated, because that sentence is the one that has to be
 * understood by someone who cannot read the rest.
 */
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Band from '@/components/Band.astro';
import Display from '@/components/Display.astro';
import Prose from '@/components/Prose.astro';
import { getChrome } from '@/i18n/chrome';
import { getMuqunCopy } from '@/i18n/muqun';
import { supportEmail } from '@/lib/muqun-links';
import { siteLocaleCodes, defaultLocale } from '@/lib/locales';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return siteLocaleCodes.map((code) => ({
    params: { lang: code === defaultLocale ? undefined : code },
    props: { locale: code },
  }));
}

/**
 * `locale` is inferred from `getStaticPaths` above rather than declared as a
 * `Props` interface: with both, the interface is the one TypeScript ignores,
 * so it is a second declaration of the same fact that nothing checks.
 */
const { locale } = Astro.props;
const chrome = getChrome(locale);

/**
 * Edited by hand when the policy is. Not a build date: a page that claims to
 * have been revised every time the site is deployed is telling the reader
 * nothing, and telling a reviewer something false.
 */
const lastUpdated = 'August 4, 2026';

/**
 * The description is the app's own, not a summary of this page: this is the
 * document that says Muqun has no account and no relay, and the app's meta
 * description is the shortest true statement of that already in eight
 * languages.
 */
const description = getMuqunCopy(locale).meta.description;
---

<Layout lang={locale} title={`${chrome.privacy.title} — ${chrome.site.name}`} description={description}>
  {/* Narrow, because this is the one page on the site that is read rather
      than scanned: 34em of measure and nothing beside it. */}
  <Band tone="cream" contain="narrow">
    <header class="mq-policy-head">
      <p class="mq-mono-label">{chrome.site.name}</p>
      <Display level={1} size="display-sm" lines={1}>{chrome.privacy.title}</Display>
      <Prose size="small">{chrome.privacy.lastUpdatedLabel}: {lastUpdated}</Prose>
      {
        /* Only rendered where it is true. On the English page it would be a
           sentence explaining that English is in English. */
        locale === defaultLocale ? null : (
          <Prose size="small" lang={locale}>
            {chrome.privacy.sourceNote}
          </Prose>
        )
      }
    </header>

    <!-- `lang="en"` on the body, because it is English inside a page whose
         document language is not: without it a screen reader reads the whole
         policy in the reader's own phonology and it is unintelligible. -->
    <Prose as="article" full lang="en">
      <p>
        Muqun is a mobile client for Gateway instances that you control, running on tmux or
        Herdr. It has no Muqun account, advertising, analytics, or tracking SDKs.
      </p>

      <h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
      <p>Muqun stores the information needed to provide its features:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Gateway names, addresses, pairing times, and access tokens.</li>
        <li>App preferences and your custom quick commands.</li>
        <li>The currently selected Gateway.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Gateway records are encrypted and stored using the operating system's secure credential
        storage. They are marked as device-only so they do not sync to another device or restore
        from a backup.
      </p>

      <h2>Gateway connections</h2>
      <p>
        Terminal sessions go directly between Muqun and the Gateway address you pair. Osuki does
        not relay, receive, or store terminal output, commands, source code, conversations,
        Gateway addresses, or access tokens.
      </p>
      <p>
        Requests to your Gateway may contain the access token, workspace and pane identifiers,
        terminal input, commands, and agent output required for the feature you use. If
        notifications are enabled, Muqun also sends the Gateway a device notification token,
        device name, and platform.
      </p>
      <p>
        You control the Gateway and its network. We strongly recommend running Muqun and the
        Gateway on the same <a href="https://tailscale.com/">Tailscale</a>
        tailnet, preferably with Tailscale Serve providing a private HTTPS address. Muqun supports
        HTTP on user-managed private networks, but HTTP itself does not encrypt traffic; only use
        it inside a trusted encrypted network such as Tailscale.
      </p>

      <h2>Camera and App Lock</h2>
      <p>
        Camera access is requested only when you open the QR scanner. Frames are processed on the
        device to read a pairing code and are not saved or uploaded. You can pair by entering a
        Gateway address manually instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        If App Lock is enabled, the operating system performs Face ID, Touch ID, fingerprint, face
        unlock, or passcode authentication. Muqun receives only whether authentication succeeded
        and never receives or stores biometric data or your passcode.
      </p>

      <h2>Notifications</h2>
      <p>
        Gateway notifications are optional. When enabled, the notification service processes the
        device notification token, platform, minimal notification content, and delivery metadata
        needed to deliver the alert. This information is used only for app functionality, not
        advertising or tracking. Turning notifications off unregisters the device from the paired
        Gateway.
      </p>

      <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
      <p>
        Local Gateway data remains until you remove that Gateway or uninstall Muqun. App
        preferences and quick commands remain until changed or the app is uninstalled.
        Gateway-side data is controlled by the Gateway operator; revoke a device from the Gateway
        to invalidate its token.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you contact Osuki for support, we use the information you provide only to respond to
        your request.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
      <p>
        We may update this policy when Muqun's features or service providers change. The current
        version and revision date will remain available at this URL.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy may be sent to
        <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </Band>
</Layout>

<style>
  .mq-policy-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--mq-space-4);
    margin-block-end: var(--mq-space-12);
    padding-block-end: var(--mq-space-8);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--mq-hairline);
  }
</style>
